<div class="article-body">

<p>Here is how to harvest lettuce so it keeps growing: cut about one inch above the crown, leave the small leaves in the middle, and never take more than a third of the plant in one session. That is the whole rule. Lettuce pushes new leaves from the crown, not from the cut, so everything depends on what you leave standing.</p>

<p>I spent a full season getting that wrong. Twelve plants on one shelf, four per method, and a $12 kitchen scale next to the tray because I wanted weights instead of opinions. The method that looked the most productive gave me the least lettuce. The one that looked like a waste of time won by 60 grams per plant.</p>

<p>Here is the part nobody puts in the video: a lettuce plant is not a paycheck you cash every week. It is a prepaid card. Every cut spends the balance, and the balance is set the day you start the seed.</p>

<h2 id="answer">1. The Short Answer: One Inch, One Third</h2>

<p>Match the method to the type of lettuce you planted. This is the part that decides whether you get one dinner or nine.</p>

