<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> crisphead lettuce is a single tight head built around one growing point. Cut it and there is nothing left to push new leaves. Loose-leaf types are a rosette of separate leaves around a low crown that holds spare buds. That is why the same advice sounds like magic for one plant and does nothing for the other.</p>

<p>For the loose-leaf plants, three numbers cover the cut: one inch of stem left above the crown, one third of the leaf mass removed at most, and leaves at four to six inches tall before you start. Colorado State Extension gives the same one inch of leafy growth left behind for container greens, and NoSoil Solutions says do not cut into the heart. Same number from two directions.</p>

<h2 id="crown">2. What the Crown Actually Is</h2>

<p>Get down at tray level and look at the base of a lettuce plant. There is a short fat stem at the water line, then a tight cluster of small leaves in the middle, then the big outer leaves fanning out. The middle cluster is the growing tip. The ring around it is older tissue.</p>

<p>When you chop a loose-leaf plant, you are not killing it. You are removing the leaves and leaving the crown, and the buds sitting in that crown push out replacements. This is the same reason basil recovers from a hard pinch and mint refuses to die.</p>

<p><strong>Pay attention to this number: one inch.</strong> Below that, the knife starts taking buds instead of leaves. Above it, you leave a stump tall enough to rot against a wet lid before it can push anything. One inch is the width of a knuckle. It is the entire difference between a plant that feeds you nine times and a brown stub floating in a lid.</p>

<p>Two things have to stay true for regrowth: the crown must survive, and some leaf must stay on the plant. A stub with zero leaves has no way to make sugar. It sits in the water using up whatever the roots stored until that runs out too.</p>

<h2 id="test">3. Twelve Plants, One Shelf, One Scale</h2>

<p>I ran this on a single wire shelf, 14 hours of light, 210 PPFD at the tray, EC at 1.4 and reservoir at 68 to 71F. Twelve Black Seeded Simpson plants in 2 inch net cups, four per method, same batch of seed and the same light bar. Every harvest went on the scale before it went in the spinner.</p>

<p>The three methods were the ones every article recommends. Method A: chop the whole plant one inch above the crown. Method B: chop three inches above the crown, which is the taller cut some garden writers suggest. Method C: pick the three or four biggest outer leaves and leave everything else alone.</p>

<p>Method A gave me the heaviest single cut of the whole test, 74 grams in one go. That plant finished at 154 grams over three cuts and bolted on day 38. Method B did 188 grams over four cuts. Method C gave 214 grams across nine small picks and was still producing on day 56, when I finally pulled it because I wanted the shelf back.</p>

<p>Total across all twelve plants: 556 grams of lettuce from 12 seeds and about $3 of nutrients. The biggest single harvest belonged to the method that produced the least food.</p>

<h2 id="card">4. The Prepaid Card Model</h2>

<p>Here is the mental model that made the numbers make sense to me. A lettuce plant is a prepaid card with a fixed balance. New growth is paid for out of that balance, and there are only three ways to spend it: leaves you cut, energy the plant cannot make because you took its solar panels, and root reserves it burns to rebuild.</p>

<p>Chop the whole plant and you withdraw everything at once. The plant now has to rebuild leaf area from stored sugar, which is slow, and the stress pushes it toward bolting, which closes the account. Pick three outer leaves and you withdraw a small amount while the rest of the plant keeps making new deposits all day under the light.</p>

<p>This is why the small-harvest method wins on total grams even though each pick feels like nothing. It is also why the two big numbers people quote for harvest counts, three to five cuts and six to ten cuts, can both be correct. They are not describing two techniques. They are describing two different amounts of time before the plant bolts.</p>

<h2 id="methods">5. Three Methods, Weighed</h2>

<p>Same seed, same light, same reservoir. The only thing that changed was where the blade went.</p>

