<p>Method B beat A for a boring reason: the taller cut left leaf area behind, and leaf area is the engine. But B also left a 3 inch stump sitting in a wet lid, and two of those four plants grew a soft brown patch on the cut face by week three. In soil that stump dries out. In a hydroponic tray under a lid it stays humid and close to the water, and that is a different problem.</p>

<p>So the tall cut is not free. It buys speed and costs you rot risk, which is exactly the kind of trade a soil guide cannot warn you about.</p>

<h2 id="race">6. Your Knife Is Not the Limit</h2>

<p>I thought the cut was the whole story. Then I put four plants in a bucket that ran at 79F because the pump and the light bar were heating 2 gallons of water in a closed room.</p>

<p>Those four plants bolted on day 31. Two cuts each, then a flower stalk and bitter leaves. Same seed, same knife, same light, same feed as the shelf at 68F. The difference was 11 degrees of water and 27 fewer days of production.</p>

<p><strong>This is where it gets interesting.</strong> Harvest count is not a skill. It is a race between two clocks. Your regrowth clock is the 8 to 13 days the plant needs to push a usable set of leaves again. Your bolt clock is the 31 to 60 days the plant has before heat and stress turn it into a seed factory. Cuts per plant is just the first clock divided into the second.</p>

<p>That single idea explains every contradictory number on the internet. A grower with a cool reservoir in a basement reports six to ten picks. A grower with a warm bucket in a spare room reports three to five. Both counted honestly.</p>

<p>And it changes what you should do after reading this. If you want more harvests, do not buy a fancier knife. Cool the water, hold EC at 1.2 to 1.6, keep the light at 14 hours, and pick the plant before it stretches. The <a href="/posts/hydroponic-lettuce-bitter">bitter lettuce article</a> covers the temperature and EC numbers, and the <a href="/posts/why-is-my-lettuce-bolting-indoors">bolting guide</a> covers what the stalk looks like before you notice it.</p>

<h2 id="cut">7. The Cut Itself: Angle, Tool, Hygiene</h2>

<p>Once the setup is right, the cut is 20 seconds of work. Do it in this order.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Cut one inch above the crown, on an angle.</strong> A slanted cut sheds water instead of pooling it on the wound, and pooled water on a cut stem is how rot starts.</li>
  <li><strong>One clean slice, not a saw.</strong> A serrated blade dragged back and forth crushes the stem. Crushed tissue goes brown before it goes green again.</li>
  <li><strong>Wipe the blade between plants.</strong> Rubbing alcohol on a paper towel, or the one part vinegar to nine parts water mix some growers use. Thirty seconds, and it stops you carrying rot from a sick plant to eight healthy ones.</li>
  <li><strong>Take from the outside in.</strong> The outer leaves are the oldest tissue and the plant has already stopped investing in them. The inner leaves are the ones still expanding.</li>
  <li><strong>Stop at one third.</strong> Every guide agrees, and my scale agrees. More than a third and the next cut arrives two days later and lighter.</li>
</ul>

<p>A sharp paring knife or a pair of harvest snips both work. Scissors are fine for outer leaves and clumsy for a whole-head chop because you end up doing three bites. Whatever you use, sharpen it once a season. A dull blade is the most common reason a stump looks shredded instead of sliced.</p>

<p>The harvest hour gets argued about more than it deserves. Pick in the morning and the leaves are full of water and crisp. Pick at the end of the light period and they carry more sugar, which is what the afternoon harvest measurement in crisphead lettuce found. For regrowth the hour makes no difference at all, because the plant is spending the same crown either way. Pick when you will actually eat, then get the leaves cold within the hour.</p>

<h2 id="after">8. The 48 Hours After the Cut</h2>

<p>The harvest does not end at the blade. Two days after a cut decide whether you get another one.</p>

<p>First, the timeline so you know what normal looks like. A loose-leaf plant needs 8 to 13 days at 14 hours of light to give you a usable set of leaves again. Five to seven days gets you a handful of baby leaves, not a salad. A romaine stump will push salad leaves in 12 to 15 days, and then it bolts instead of making another head. If your tray is sitting at 20 days with nothing, the problem is the crown, the water, or the light, not patience.</p>

<p><strong>Keep the light on schedule.</strong> The plant has less leaf area, so it needs the same hours to rebuild. Dropping to 10 hours because the tray looks empty slows the recovery by days. Hold your normal 14 hours and your normal distance.</p>

<p><strong>Do not push the feed.</strong> The instinct is to add nutrients because the plant lost mass. That is backwards. A plant with less leaf area cannot use a stronger solution, and high EC is one of the stress signals that pushes lettuce toward bolting. Hold EC where it was, 1.2 to 1.6.</p>

<p><strong>Drop the water level a half inch.</strong> The roots still work, but a cut crown sitting in splash is a rot site. If your net cup sits low in the lid, this is the week to raise it slightly.</p>

<p><strong>Move air across the cut faces.</strong> A small clip fan on low for a few hours a day dries the wound without drying the tray. This is the cheapest insurance in the whole process, and the same airflow that prevents tipburn pays for itself here too. The <a href="/posts/how-much-light-does-lettuce-need">light and airflow numbers</a> explain why moving air changes so much on an indoor shelf.</p>

<p><strong>Clean up the trimmings.</strong> Leaves that fall into the reservoir feed algae and fungus gnats. Pull them out the same day, not next weekend.</p>

<h2 id="dead">9. When the Stump Stops Regrowing</h2>

<p>Three failures account for almost every dead stump I have grown.</p>

<p><strong>The cut was too low.</strong> The crown is gone, or the buds got sliced. Look at the stump: if it is a smooth disc the width of the stem, the crown went with the harvest. Nothing will come back from that.</p>

<p><strong>No leaves were left.</strong> A bare stub cannot photosynthesize. It will sometimes push two tiny pale leaves and then stall for a week before dying. Leaving two or three inner leaves solves it, and it costs you maybe 4 grams of salad.</p>

<p><strong>The cut face rotted.</strong> Soft brown tissue, a sour smell, fungus gnats hovering. This is the warm water and wet lid combination. Cut higher next time, dry the wound with airflow, and keep the reservoir under 72F.</p>

<p>There is one more case people ask about, and it is worth separating: regrowing the stump of a supermarket lettuce in a glass of water. It works, but only for one round of small leaves, and it always bolts before it makes a real head. There are no new nutrients in tap water, so the plant is spending the same prepaid balance with none of the deposits. It is a fun kitchen trick. It is not a lettuce supply.</p>

<h2 id="setup">10. The Setup, and the Honest Truth</h2>

<p>Nothing here is expensive. The three things that made this test possible cost less than one seed packet order.</p>

<p><strong>The trap.</strong> A $60 hydroponic "harvest kit" with a plastic blade, a spray bottle, and a booklet. The blade is dull in a month and the booklet is the same four paragraphs every blog has.</p>

<p><strong>The overkill.</strong> A commercial lettuce harvester or a full set of stainless chef knives. You are cutting stems the width of a pencil.</p>

<p><strong>What I actually use.</strong> A <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=lettuce+harvest+knife+stainless+steel&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">thin stainless harvest knife</a> for whole-head cuts, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=garden+harvest+snips+stainless+steel&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">pair of harvest snips</a> for outer leaves, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=digital+kitchen+scale+0.1g+precision&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">0.1 gram kitchen scale</a> for weighing, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=salad+spinner+large+5+quart&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">salad spinner</a> for the wash, and a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mechanical+outlet+timer+plug+15+minute&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">mechanical outlet timer</a> so the 14 hours never drifts. Around $45 for the lot, and the scale is the only one I would fight you about. Without it you are guessing, and guessing is how I lost a season. That scale runs about $12 and bounces between $9 and $16 depending on the week. Under $13 is a solid buy, and any 0.1 gram kitchen scale will do the same job.</p>

<p>Here is the honest truth about the harvest knife. The blade is thin, which is exactly what makes it clean on a lettuce stem, and it also flexes. On a crowded 1020 tray you cannot get a straight push through a whole head without bending the tip, so I end up using the snips for tray work and the knife only for plants I can lift out. It is a good $10 knife with a real limit, and the limit is the reason I keep both tools.</p>

<p>The real fix for a steady supply is not a better harvest. It is a second tray. Sow a new 1020 tray every 10 days and you stop rationing the plants you have. A <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=1020+seed+starting+tray+no+holes&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">1020 tray with no holes</a> costs about $4, and three of them in rotation beat any harvesting trick on this page.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> cut loose-leaf lettuce one inch above the crown, take outer leaves before you ever take a whole head, never remove more than a third, and leave two or three small leaves to keep the plant fed. My twelve plants proved the small picks win, 214 grams to 154, and the number of harvests behind that total was decided by water temperature, not by blade angle. If you are buying seed for the next round, the <a href="/posts/best-lettuce-varieties-for-indoors">lettuce varieties that regrow after cutting</a> covers which ones actually come back, the <a href="/posts/grow-lettuce-indoors-year-round">year round indoor lettuce guide</a> has the seed to cut timeline, and the <a href="/grow-lights">grow light database</a> lists real coverage and price for the bars worth running 14 hours a day. My first season of stumps went in the bin so yours do not have to. Let my twelve plants take the hit instead of yours.</p>

